fig.test.ts:136:  test(\"operation scheduling policy reads validated operator settings\", () => {\norigin/main:cli/test/config.test.ts:164:        operationPolicy: { maxWaitMs: 60_000, maxRunsPerUnit: 4, minWaitMs: 250 },\norigin/main:cli/test/config.test.ts:167:      fs.writeFileSync(path.join(workspace, \"nexus.config.json\"), JSON.stringify({\norigin/main:cli/test/config.test.ts:169:        operationPolicy: { maxWaitMs: 1000, maxRunsPerUnit: 6 },\norigin/main:cli/test/config.test.ts:209:      fs.writeFileSync(configFile, JSON.stringify({ version: 1, operationPolicy: { maxRunsPerUnit: 1.5 } }));\norigin/main:cli/test/config.test.ts:212:      assert.match(invalidValue.stderr, /Invalid Nexus config\\.json operationPolicy\\.maxRunsPerUnit/);\norigin/main:cli/test/config.test.ts:214:      fs.writeFileSync(configFile, JSON.stringify({ version: 1, operationPolicy: { maxWaitMs: 2_147_483_648 } }));\norigin/main:cli/test/config.test.ts:224:      fs.writeFileSync(target, JSON.stringify({ version: 1, operationPolicy: { maxRunsPerUnit: 2 } }));\norigin/main:cli/test/config.test.ts:236:  test(\"operation scheduling policy rejects invalid operator values\", () => {\norigin/main:cli/test/config.test.ts:275:  test(\"operation spec overrides are validated after merging with operator policy\", () => {\norigin/main:cli/test/config.test.ts:288:        /Invalid operation policy/,\norigin/main:cli/test/config.test.ts:294:      /Invalid operation policy unknownResumeBudget/,\norigin/main:cli/test/operator_config_startup.test.ts:70:  withOperatorConfig(JSON.stringify({ version: 1, operationPolicy: { hiddenDefault: 2 } }), (home) => {\norigin/main:cli/test/operator_config_startup.test.ts:76:    assert.match(finding.evidence[0], /Unsupported Nexus config\\.json operationPolicy field 'hiddenDefault'/);\norigin/main:cli/test/operator_config_startup.test.ts:101:  withOperatorConfig(JSON.stringify({ version: 1, operationPolicy: { maxWaitMs: 0 } }), (home) => {\norigin/main:cli/test/operator_config_startup.test.ts:104:    assert.match(result.stderr, /Configuration error: Invalid Nexus config\\.json operationPolicy\\.maxWaitMs/);\norigin/main:cli/test/team_convergence.test.ts:17:import { DecisionRejected, newOperation, type DurableOperationState, type OperationEvent, type OperationEventType, type UnitRunResult, type WorkUnit } from \"../src/core/operation.js\";\norigin/main:cli/test/team_convergence.test.ts:194:  test(\"operator operation policy is persisted when planning a team operation\", async () => {\norigin/main:cli/test/team_convergence.test.ts:205:      operationPolicy: { maxWaitMs: 3_600_000, unknownResumeBudget: 0, maxRunsPerUnit: 8, minWaitMs: 250 },\norigin/main:cli/test/team_convergence.test.ts:920:      const tryApply = (d: string, o: string, note?: string) => assert.throws(() => applyOperatorDecision(env.store, \"team-dec\", d, o, note), DecisionRejected);\norigin/main:docs/EXECUTION_CONVERGENCE.md:98:the option id and the action against current state and throws `DecisionRejected` otherwise; applying one records\norigin/main:docs/ISSUE12_FINAL_ACCEPTANCE_MATRIX.md:31:| CAP-007 | 02: Capability Identity | A canonical capability MAY have multiple declared execution backends only when each backend satisfies the identical operation contract and domain policy. Switching backends MUST NOT silently alter execution authority or evidence records. | Executing a command via direct fork/exec or via Bubblewrap sandbox MUST adhere to the same execution status and timeout contract. | Phase 2 §6 | `cli/src/core/spine.ts`; cli/test/capabilities.test.ts | DEMONSTRATED | None | Satisfied |\norigin/main:docs/ISSUE12_FINAL_ACCEPTANCE_MATRIX.md:32:| AUTH-001 | 03: Authority | Authority MUST be evaluated against the concrete invocation (operation, effective resource, destination, conditions, actor, and execution domain) at the time of effect. Tool possession or capability presence MUST NOT confer invocation authority. | Having access to `linux_command_execute` does not authorize executing commands outside the configured command/argument policy. | Phase 2 §7 | `cli/src/core/authority.ts`; cli/test/authority.test.ts, cli/test/system_journey.test.ts | DEMONSTRATED | None | Satisfied |\norigin/main:docs/PHASE2_RECONCILED_REQUIREMENTS.md:147:| **CAP-007** | A canonical capability MAY have multiple declared execution backends only when each backend satisfies the identical operation contract and domain policy. Switching backends MUST NOT silently alter execution authority or evidence records. | Executing a command via direct fork/exec or via Bubblewrap sandbox MUST adhere to the same execution status and timeout contract. |\norigin/main:docs/PHASE2_RECONCILED_REQUIREMENTS.md:178:| **AUTH-001** | Authority MUST be evaluated against the concrete invocation (operation, effective resource, destination, conditions, actor, and execution domain) at the time of effect. Tool possession or capability presence MUST NOT confer invocation authority. | Having access to `linux_command_execute` does not authorize executing commands outside the configured command/argument policy. |\norigin/main:docs/PHASE2_REQUIREMENTS_AND_PLAN.md:79:| CAP-007 | One canonical operation may have multiple explicitly declared execution backends only when each satisfies the operation contract and the domain policy; a backend change must not silently change authority or evidence. |\norigin/main:docs/PHASE3_IMPLEMENTATION_DECOMPOSITION.md:355:For local resource access across all capabilities (including file operations and `browser_navigate` with local `file://` URIs), effective target paths must be admitted against `workspaceRoot` and `allowedSubtrees` via `resolve_safe_path` / `AuthorityAdmissionEngine`. Outbound remote network requests across all capabilities (including `fetch_web`, `search_web`, and remote browser navigation) must evaluate `networkAllowed` and policy domain allowlists before egress (`DEST-001`, `DEST-002`).\norigin/main:docs/USER_MANUAL.md:360:### Durable operation scheduling policy\norigin/main:docs/USER_MANUAL.md:362:Operation defaults can be set in the operator-owned `~/.config/nexus/config.json`, the legacy `~/.config/nexus/config.env`, or the operator's shell environment. The initial structured config schema supports `operationPolicy`:\norigin/main:docs/USER_MANUAL.md:367:  \"operationPolicy\": {\norigin/main:docs/USER_MANUAL.md:376:The file is read only from the operator's home directory; a repository-level `nexus.config.json` cannot set operator policy. Unsupported fields, invalid values, symlinks and files larger than 64 KiB are rejected. Values resolve from the shell environment first, then `config.json`, then `config.env`, then built-in defaults. Existing `config.env` installations remain supported.\norigin/main:docs/reviews/NEXUS_APPLICATION_MATURITY_REVIEW_2026-10-12.md:13:**Overall verdict: strong feature and internal-control maturity; operational/product maturity remains unproven.** Highest priorities are (1) define and enforce a deliberate trust/egress policy, (2) validate packaging and release artifacts from clean builds, (3) strengthen migration/compatibility and recovery testing, and (4) establish measurable reliability/telemetry and support practices. These are gaps or evidence gaps, not assertions that every mechanism is absent.\norigin/main:docs/reviews/evidence/v1.1/CONTEXT-MANAGEMENT-20261028/README.md:94:- `contextBudget` is per unit only; there is no operation-level or policy default, and no automatic sizing from task\norigin/main:docs/v1.0.0/READINESS.md:67:**Requested codebase-review findings:** The prior checkpoint's implementation and evidence summary remains applicable; the current main includes the capability-category split, scheduler extraction, operation-runner decomposition, bounded diagnostics, `DecisionRejected` guidance, lifecycle tests, and 100 fixed-seed scheduler invariants. The operator config remains intentionally limited to `operationPolicy`; general runtime-default customization is not claimed.\norigin/main:docs/v1.0.0/READINESS.md:81:**Requested codebase-review findings are part of the v1.0.0 scope:** The large capability registry is split into category modules (`cli/src/core/capabilities.ts`, 10,046 bytes, plus `capability_definitions/`); scheduler logic is in `cli/src/core/scheduler.ts` (23,696 bytes), with `operation.ts` reduced to 12,818 bytes; runner revalidation, execution, and persistence are separated from `operation_runner.ts` (14,931 bytes). Runner decomposition and bounded revalidation/runner diagnostics were integrated through PR [#287](https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/pull/287); TDD evidence diagnostics landed through PR [#295](https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/pull/295), and scheduler observer diagnostics through PR [#296](https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/pull/296). PR [#297](https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/pull/297), now merged, adds regression coverage for the TDD diagnostic paths. The capability split and scheduler extraction were integrated through PRs [#286](https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/pull/286) and [#285](https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/pull/285), respectively. PR [#293](https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/pull/293) added operator guidance for `DecisionRejected` and is merged with its Node 22/24, Linux, and CodeQL checks successful. These source changes are implemented on current `main`.\norigin/main:docs/v1.0.0/READINESS.md:85:**Operator configuration scope:** `~/.config/nexus/config.json` is the operator-owned structured configuration entry point for `operationPolicy`, with schema validation, bounded file size, symlink rejection, and environment/config/default precedence documented in `docs/USER_MANUAL.md`. The schema is intentionally limited to that policy today; this record does not claim every runtime default is configurable or that a repository-level config can override operator policy.\norigin/main:docs/v1.0.0/READINESS.md